BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:97f3fb121ca63928@kidcal
DTSTAMP:20261113T182525Z
DTSTART:20270713T220000Z
DTEND:20270713T230000Z
SUMMARY:Why am I Fatiqued?
LOCATION:Miller-Vincent Room
URL:https://keenenh.libcal.com/event/17724150
CATEGORIES:kids\,Keene Public Library
DESCRIPTION:Lior Sadeh of Three Fold Herbal Healing presents: Why am I Fat
 igued?\n\nFatigue is one of the most common concerns people bring to my he
 rbal clinic\, yet uncovering why you feel persistently drained can be chal
 lenging. From nutrient deficiencies and sleep disruptions to thyroid imbal
 ances\, chronic stress\, and conditions such as chronic fatigue syndrome\,
  fatigue can have many underlying causes.\n\nJoin us at the library for a 
 practical\, informative community session exploring common contributors to
  fatigue\, important questions to discuss with your healthcare provider\, 
 and everyday lifestyle strategies to support your energy and overall well 
 being. You’ll leave with a clearer understanding of what may be contribu
 ting to your tiredness and actionable steps to help you feel your best. Re
 gistration is Required.\n\nLior Sadeh\, is a clinical herbalist who weaves
  modern\, evidence-based knowledge with traditional Western herbalism. As 
 a "retired" farmer with over a decade of experience in herb growing and me
 dicine-making\, she has developed an intimate relationship with many of th
 e herbs she uses in her practice.\n\nLior practices root cause herbalism. 
 One phrase that you will often hear her say is\, "It's Not what you take\;
  it's what you Do."\n\nThrough her workshops she hopes you gain a better u
 nderstanding your body's language\, so you can:\n\n\n	Advocate for your he
 alth by asking the right questions when you visit your PCP\n	Make small st
 eps toward a lifestyle better suited for your body while being supported e
 very step of the way.\nSource: Keene Public Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f8ba914c0d9608f6@kidcal
DTSTAMP:20261113T182525Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T190000
SUMMARY:BETTER OFF READ: It's About Time...
LOCATION:The DBA Garage (former First Prood Press space)\, 22 High Street\
 , Brattleboro\, 05301\, United States
URL:https://brookslibraryvt.org/event/better-off-read-its-about-time/
CATEGORIES:kids\,Brooks Memorial Library
DESCRIPTION:Readings & Conversations with Nico Amador\, Cameron Awkward-Ri
 ch\, and Stephanie Burt \nAt The DBA Garage at 22 High Street (former Firs
 t Proof Press space) Brattleboro\, VT  05301 \nJoin us for another sparkli
 ng evening of trans literature and conversation! In partnership with Every
 one’s Books and Brooks Memorial Library\, Out in the Open is thrilled to
  host prize-winning poets Nico Amador\, Cameron Awkward-Rich\, and Stephan
 ie Burt at a beautiful new event space at 22 High Street. There will be so
 up\, bread\, discussion\, books for sale\, and more. Free and open to the 
 public. \nThis program is supported by a Brattleboro Town Arts Fund grant 
 – and is presented by Out in the Open\, Everyone’s Books\, & Brooks Me
 morial Library.\n————— \nNico Amador is a writer and educator fr
 om San Diego\, currently based in Vermont and Provincetown. Informed by hi
 s involvement in movements for social change\, his poetry is interested in
  acts of radical imagination\, place\, and belonging.  \nCameron Awkward-R
 ich is a poet\, professor\, and the author of three poetry collections\, i
 ncluding An Optimism— “a book that un-mutes possibility [and] re-imagi
 nes time\,” winner of the 2026 Massachusetts Book Award. His latest proj
 ect\, Time Also Will Make It Interesting\, is an edited\,”queerly messy
 ” selection of the journals of writer/painter/activist Red Jordan Arobat
 eau\, a crucial yet undersung figure in San Francisco trans queer cultures
 . \nStephanie Burt is a poet\, literary critic\, professor\, and author of
  14 books\, including We Are Mermaids—a poetry collection “living in t
 he trans present and future.” Burt’s groundbreaking anthology Super Ga
 y Poems: LGBTQIA+ Poetry After Stonewall won the 2025 New England Book awa
 rd and has been acclaimed as “not just a guide to queer poetry\, but a n
 ew way of thinking about beauty\, passion\, and the passage of time.\nSour
 ce: Brooks Memorial Library
END:VEVENT
